1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
[serious] About $558,100 in paper profit,
more than a couple can take tax-free.

2
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:13,280
That's Rosa and Frank, an illustrative
Phoenix couple who bought in 2000,

3
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:15,360
if their home rose like the average.

4
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:20,480
You'll know, city by city, the 2000 price
where a home that rose like its metro

5
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:24,720
area's average passes that cap, to check
yours against.

6
00:00:25,133 --> 00:00:28,813
It's a line for a home like the average:
not anyone's tax bill,

7
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:31,213
and not a reason to sell or keep.

8
00:00:31,389 --> 00:00:36,413
This is US only: federal tax, with state
taxes not modeled.

9
00:00:36,546 --> 00:00:41,213
Every couple here is illustrative, and
assumed to have owned and lived in the

10
00:00:41,357 --> 00:00:44,413
home two of the five years before selling.

11
00:00:44,573 --> 00:00:48,573
And every price you'll see is history, not
a forecast.

12
00:00:49,367 --> 00:00:53,607
When you sell, your gain is the sale
price, less what you paid,

13
00:00:53,727 --> 00:00:56,327
and less what you spent improving the
place.

14
00:00:56,607 --> 00:01:02,167
A married couple filing jointly can leave
up to $500,000 of that gain off their

15
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:07,367
taxes, and a single seller can leave out
$250,000.

16
00:01:07,554 --> 00:01:11,767
Those caps took effect in May 1997.

17
00:01:11,895 --> 00:01:16,007
They are plain dollar amounts, and they
haven't changed since.

18
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:17,720
Back to Frank and Rosa.

19
00:01:18,173 --> 00:01:22,920
In 2000, they paid $200,000 for a house in
Phoenix.

20
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,560
Their kids have moved out, and on weekends
they both look at smaller places.

21
00:01:27,649 --> 00:01:31,480
Whenever selling comes up, Frank says the
same thing:

22
00:01:31,533 --> 00:01:33,720
at least the profit is tax-free.

23
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:38,280
[thoughtful] Is it? Has their gain passed
the cap?

24
00:01:39,267 --> 00:01:43,747
We can't see their house, so we let its
value rise exactly like the Phoenix area

25
00:01:43,795 --> 00:01:47,907
home price index from the Federal Housing
Finance Agency,

26
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:49,587
an average of many sales.

27
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:55,427
We follow it quarter by quarter, from 2000
to the second quarter of 2026,

28
00:01:55,447 --> 00:01:56,467
the latest data.

29
00:01:56,867 --> 00:02:02,787
Here's the cap: a flat line at $500,000,
the same in every quarter.

30
00:02:03,007 --> 00:02:07,827
And here's their gain on paper, for a home
that rose like the average:

31
00:02:07,847 --> 00:02:14,847
the $200,000, grown with the index, less
the $200,000 they paid.

32
00:02:15,427 --> 00:02:18,947
For most of these years, it sits well
under the line.

33
00:02:19,059 --> 00:02:23,027
Then, in the second quarter of 2022, it
crosses.

34
00:02:23,294 --> 00:02:25,587
It slips back under for a while.

35
00:02:25,683 --> 00:02:30,067
From the second quarter of 2023, it has
stayed above.

36
00:02:31,067 --> 00:02:34,507
[serious] So, on paper, if their home rose
like the Phoenix average,

37
00:02:34,571 --> 00:02:36,267
this is the gain from the opening.

38
00:02:36,443 --> 00:02:38,707
That's past the cap.

39
00:02:38,792 --> 00:02:43,307
Phoenix area prices are now almost 3.8
times their 2000 level.

40
00:02:43,387 --> 00:02:48,483
And it's a gain, not a tax bill:
improvements and selling costs would both

41
00:02:48,484 --> 00:02:49,486
shrink it.

42
00:02:49,487 --> 00:02:53,787
The cap stood still while their gain
climbed past it.

43
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:59,307
So is Phoenix unusual, or would the same
thing happen in other cities?

44
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:02,641
First, why is the line flat?

45
00:03:02,921 --> 00:03:05,681
The cap is written as a plain dollar
amount,

46
00:03:05,741 --> 00:03:07,441
and it has never been adjusted.

47
00:03:07,681 --> 00:03:13,921
If $500,000 from May 1997 had kept up with
consumer prices,

48
00:03:13,974 --> 00:03:19,841
it would be about $1,046,000 in August
2026 dollars.

49
00:03:20,134 --> 00:03:25,681
It's everyday prices, not house prices,
but it shows why a cap that never moves

50
00:03:25,772 --> 00:03:29,361
covers less and less of a long-held home's
gain.

51
00:03:30,567 --> 00:03:34,087
Now run the same replay in 12 big metro
areas:

52
00:03:34,137 --> 00:03:39,607
illustrative couples, each paying $200,000
in 2000 for a home that rose like its

53
00:03:39,674 --> 00:03:41,367
metro area's average.

54
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:47,287
By the second quarter of 2026, the gain is
past the cap in 5 of them.

55
00:03:47,427 --> 00:03:51,767
Los Angeles, San Diego, Seattle, Miami and
Phoenix.

56
00:03:52,367 --> 00:03:55,327
Now give every couple $300,000 instead.

57
00:03:55,423 --> 00:03:59,487
This time, for homes that rose like their
metro area's average,

58
00:03:59,507 --> 00:04:02,607
the gain is past the cap in 11 of the 12.

59
00:04:02,778 --> 00:04:09,687
Boston, New York, Denver, Dallas and both
Bay Area markets join the first five.

60
00:04:09,791 --> 00:04:13,007
Only Chicago hasn't reached the cap, at
either price.

61
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:16,280
[curious] And crossing isn't always a
one-way trip.

62
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:23,480
In Miami, a $300,000 home that rose like
the average first passed the cap in the

63
00:04:23,554 --> 00:04:25,600
fourth quarter of 2006.

64
00:04:25,728 --> 00:04:30,960
Then it fell back under, and has only
stayed above since the second quarter of

65
00:04:30,994 --> 00:04:32,160
2019.

66
00:04:32,307 --> 00:04:37,440
Being past the line once, on paper, didn't
mean staying past it.

67
00:04:38,433 --> 00:04:42,753
So Phoenix isn't unusual, and it isn't
only the coasts:

68
00:04:42,810 --> 00:04:44,913
Dallas and Denver crossed too.

69
00:04:45,286 --> 00:04:46,913
So flip the question.

70
00:04:47,057 --> 00:04:52,513
What 2000 purchase price would put a home
that rose like its metro area's average

71
00:04:52,673 --> 00:04:54,913
exactly at the cap now?

72
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:59,220
In Phoenix, for a home that rose like the
average,

73
00:04:59,252 --> 00:05:02,980
that price is about $179,200.

74
00:05:03,172 --> 00:05:09,060
Rosa and Frank paid more than that, and
that's why their line crossed.

75
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:13,140
Below that price, a Phoenix home is still
under the cap;

76
00:05:13,193 --> 00:05:14,740
above it, it's over.

77
00:05:15,933 --> 00:05:20,733
Do that for all 12 metro areas, each for a
home that rose like its metro area's

78
00:05:20,783 --> 00:05:23,453
average, and you get a ladder of prices.

79
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:28,013
Miami sits at the bottom of the ladder,
and Chicago at the top.

80
00:05:28,193 --> 00:05:32,093
The faster an area's prices rose, the
lower its rung:

81
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:35,453
Miami's rose the most, Chicago's the
least.

82
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:40,573
Every city except Chicago sits under
$300,000.

83
00:05:40,653 --> 00:05:44,493
That's the cap, read a third way: not as a
flat line,

84
00:05:44,613 --> 00:05:48,573
but as a price paid in 2000 that anyone
can look up.

85
00:05:50,067 --> 00:05:55,827
So, back to the opening question: is
selling a home bought in 2000 still fully

86
00:05:55,947 --> 00:05:56,947
tax-free?

87
00:05:57,427 --> 00:06:02,867
[serious] For Rosa and Frank, if their
house rose like its metro area's average,

88
00:06:03,007 --> 00:06:06,627
not fully: part of their gain sits above
the cap.

89
00:06:06,846 --> 00:06:10,147
For you, it may come down to one
comparison:

90
00:06:10,179 --> 00:06:13,026
your 2000 price against your city's rung.

91
00:06:13,134 --> 00:06:18,342
Above that rung, a home like that has
already passed the cap. Below it,

92
00:06:18,343 --> 00:06:19,345
it hasn't.

93
00:06:19,346 --> 00:06:25,907
Below the rung isn't proof of no tax, and
above it isn't proof of a large one. The

94
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:30,147
video measures one line; it says nothing
about what anyone does next.

95
00:06:30,733 --> 00:06:33,373
Your side of it is what you paid plus
improvements,

96
00:06:33,400 --> 00:06:35,933
which count toward what the gain is
measured from.

97
00:06:36,043 --> 00:06:40,173
Selling costs, like agent fees, come off
the gain too.

98
00:06:40,293 --> 00:06:44,013
Outside these cities, the national index
puts the line,

99
00:06:44,093 --> 00:06:49,453
for a home like the average, at about
$243,500.

100
00:06:49,593 --> 00:06:56,593
For a single seller, with the $250,000
cap, the national line is about $121,700.

101
00:07:00,067 --> 00:07:01,747
None of this is anyone's tax bill.

102
00:07:01,906 --> 00:07:05,507
Gain above the cap is taxed as a long-term
capital gain,

103
00:07:05,614 --> 00:07:10,503
since the home was held more than a year,
at a rate that depends on income and

104
00:07:10,504 --> 00:07:11,506
state.

105
00:07:11,507 --> 00:07:14,947
And the average is not your home: your
street,

106
00:07:14,963 --> 00:07:19,346
your remodel and your purchase month can
put you far from your rung.

107
00:07:19,466 --> 00:07:24,307
The line is a measurement, not a next
step; this video doesn't weigh selling,

108
00:07:24,357 --> 00:07:25,747
keeping or waiting.

109
00:07:26,533 --> 00:07:29,493
How we built this is on screen and in the
description.

110
00:07:29,773 --> 00:07:32,853
The price indexes are revised every
quarter,

111
00:07:32,906 --> 00:07:35,493
so these lines can move a little with the
next release.

112
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:43,600
This is history, not a forecast: where
prices went from 2000 to the second

113
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:47,840
quarter of 2026, not where they, or the
cap,

114
00:07:47,893 --> 00:07:48,893
go next.

115
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:52,805
[thoughtful] The cap is still the same
flat line it was in 1997.

116
00:07:52,965 --> 00:07:55,605
What moved is the house underneath it.

